1
00:00:01,127 --> 00:00:06,177
Teams make promises like: every deployment becomes healthy within thirty seconds.

2
00:00:06,177 --> 00:00:08,797
That sentence hides real questions.

3
00:00:08,797 --> 00:00:15,807
Which health check counts? When does waiting turn into failure? And what does a missing event actually prove?

4
00:00:15,807 --> 00:00:23,467
Traceweaver is a tiny temporal language that makes those promises executable, replayable, and explainable, right in the browser.

5
00:00:24,991 --> 00:00:29,681
A Traceweaver program declares typed events, then promises over them.

6
00:00:29,681 --> 00:00:31,801
I'll write one from scratch.

7
00:00:31,801 --> 00:00:35,351
Deploy, Healthy and Rollback each carry a service field.

8
00:00:35,351 --> 00:00:41,631
The Ready promise says: on every Deploy, expect a Healthy event within thirty seconds, matching on service.

9
00:00:41,631 --> 00:00:49,571
Compile, and the lexer, parser and type checker accept it: three event types, one typed promise, five events from the trace.

10
00:00:51,130 --> 00:00:52,820
Now replay the trace.

11
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:56,330
At zero seconds, the API deployment is pending. Not failed, just waiting.

12
00:00:56,330 --> 00:01:03,820
Step forward. At twelve seconds, the web service is healthy, so its promise is fulfilled, while the API is still waiting.

13
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:08,950
At thirty seconds, the API window closes, and the promise is violated.

14
00:01:08,950 --> 00:01:14,100
The witness is the trigger plus a closed, complete window.

15
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:18,870
The web health check was rejected as a look-alike, because the service field differs.

16
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:23,800
And a health check that arrives later, at forty seconds, cannot repair an expired promise.

17
00:01:25,304 --> 00:01:27,984
Mistakes get located diagnostics.

18
00:01:27,984 --> 00:01:30,854
Change the unit to sec, and compile.

19
00:01:30,854 --> 00:01:37,304
Traceweaver points to the exact line and column, names the unknown unit, and lists the supported ones.

20
00:01:37,304 --> 00:01:41,104
The old results are hidden, so you never read a stale answer.

21
00:01:41,104 --> 00:01:44,464
Fix it, compile again, and the evaluation comes back.

22
00:01:46,011 --> 00:01:47,651
Experiments are portable.

23
00:01:47,651 --> 00:01:52,171
Export saves the source and the observation time, not the results.

24
00:01:52,171 --> 00:01:55,821
Switch to another story, then import the saved file.

25
00:01:55,821 --> 00:02:02,121
Traceweaver validates both sources and recomputes every obligation, ignoring any stored claims.

26
00:02:03,328 --> 00:02:06,268
The same interpreter runs from the command line.

27
00:02:06,268 --> 00:02:16,138
On the deployment example at thirty seconds, the CLI prints JSON: two violated, two fulfilled, and exit code one, because something was violated.

28
00:02:16,138 --> 00:02:24,148
Eighteen tests cover deadline boundaries, same-time ordering, wrong keys, invalid units and import validation. All passing.

29
00:02:25,706 --> 00:02:33,836
The grammar and semantics are documented in the language reference, with examples in the repository, and the workbench is live on GitHub Pages.

30
00:02:33,836 --> 00:02:37,706
Traceweaver: a small language where not yet is different from never happened.
